 "notes": [
  "Correctness: every run's full fetched-PC sequence and every store (markers, test stores and a dump of x1..x31 after each test) match a Python RV32IM ISS exactly.",
  "Memory: unmodified servant_ram behind servile_arbiter; ack (with read data) comes 1 cycle after cyc is raised, so every bus transaction takes 2 cycles. Instruction fetch is not overlapped with execution.",
  "Each instruction's cost is a fixed number of cycles, whatever comes before it: fetch-to-fetch deltas are constant and mixed sequences add up exactly. W=1: one-stage ops (ALU reg/imm, lui, auipc, all CSR ops, ecall/ebreak entry, mret) 36; two-stage ops (slt*, branches taken or not, jal/jalr, left shifts) 68; loads/stores 70; mul* 71; div/rem 103. W=4: 12 / 20 / 22 / 23 / 55.",
  "Shifts: W1: left 68 (independent of shift amount), right (srl/sra/srli/srai) 68 + shamt; W4: left 20 (independent of shift amount), right (srl/sra/srli/srai) 20 + floor(shamt/4)",
  "div/divu/rem/remu take the same time for every operand set tried (incl. divide by zero and INT_MIN/-1): the external MDU always runs a fixed 32-step divide. mul* are a 2-cycle DSP multiply plus SERV's two stages.",
  "Traps: ecall entry costs the same as one ALU op, and so does mret. Real trap cost is dominated by the handler instructions. ecall_roundtrip_min_handler = ecall + csrr mepc + addi + csrw mepc + mret.",
  "wfi is NOT a no-op on SERV: it decodes as ebreak (inst bit 20 set) and traps with mcause=3 (confirmed: mcause read back = 3). 'wfi' is the round trip through the 4-instruction handler, and wfi_trap_entry is the trap alone.",
  "FENCE / FENCE.TSO / FENCE.I are decoded as loads with no rd write: each does a real data-bus read at an address built from the instruction's immediate bits, and the address differs between W=1 and W=4. W1: {'fence': ['1-byte read at 0x8000f3ff'], 'fence_rw': ['1-byte read at 0x8000eff3'], 'fence_tso': ['1-byte read at 0x0000eff3'], 'fence_i': ['2-byte read at 0x00000000']}; W4: {'fence': ['1-byte read at 0x0ff008ff'], 'fence_rw': ['1-byte read at 0x03300833'], 'fence_tso': ['1-byte read at 0x83300833'], 'fence_i': ['2-byte read at 0x00001000']}. Several of these addresses have bit 31 set (the servile ext/peripheral region), so on a real SoC a fence can perform a read side effect on an I/O device. They cost the same as a load.",
  "Latency sensitivity (runs with 1 extra wait state on every bus transaction): cycles grow by exactly the number of bus transactions: 1 per instruction fetch, plus 1 per load/store/fence data access. So cycles(L) = cycles(table) + (L-1) * (1 + is_mem), where L = cycles from cyc to ack."
 ],
